\pard\plain \ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Jason met Kelly when she was killing herself. They were at the National Gallery, Kelly seated in front of a print of Frida Kahlo\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Broken Column. She had a pistol in her lap.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yo,\u8221\'94 Jason said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hey,\u8221\'94 Kelly said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What\u8217\'92re you doing?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Killing myself.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh,\u8221\'94 Jason said. \u8220\'93I see.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Kelly put the pistol below her chin and pulled the trigger. Brain and bone decorated the back wall like Jackson Pollock.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 The next day, Jason asked Kelly to grab a coffee at the place on the corner by the Statistics Department. When they sat down together in the morning, she poured cyanide in her macchiato. Jason watched her do it over the rim of his long black. He talked to her about his investment banking while she sipped her coffee.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 After about forty-five minutes Kelly was face down on the table between them, her macchiato drained and cup rolling on its side. Jason put her number in his phone. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93See you soon,\u8221\'94 he said, and went to have a meeting with his boss.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What got you into finance?\u8221\'94 Kelly said. She and Jason were hiking in the hills outside the city.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93My father,\u8221\'94 Jason said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What did he do?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Finance.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Kelly kicked a rock off the trail; it knocked on four different stone outcroppings on the way down to the bottom of the valley.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 When Jason told her about the new engine in his BMW, Kelly jumped from the ridge. She dived headfirst and shattered her orbital on a smooth piece of orange rock jutting from the dusty cliff face, and then broke her tibia in fourteen different places when she hit a tree at the speed of a car in a school zone.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Carry this,\u8221\'94 Jason said, and handed Kelly a box of youth rugby trophies. He walked into his new apartment, the one Kelly would be living in, too, and chucked his keys on the black marble kitchen island. Kelly plodded in behind him, trophies towering over her face.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Where do you want them?\u8221\'94 Kelly said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Put them in the bedroom,\u8221\'94 Jason said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Okay,\u8221\'94 Kelly said, and put the box in the bedroom.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 That night, after everything was moved in, and Jason was enjoying a glass of wine, Kelly went to the kitchen and took the bread knife from the knife block and ripped the serrated edge across her throat. The blood from her jugular covered the window overlooking the lake, and dripped like drizzling raindrops from the black marble.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Jason knelt in front of Kelly. It was winter, and he wore a black coat over a blue turtleneck, and she wore a thin duster with a loose, flowing skirt. There was a ring in Jason\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s hand.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes,\u8221\'94 Kelly said. \u8220\'93Of course.\u8221\'94 She bent down on the stool she was standing on and presented her hand.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 He slid the ring on her finger. He smirked and said, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Amazing,\u8221\'94 then left to check his stocks.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Kelly inspected the ring, and wondered how much it was worth. Then she kicked the stool out from under her and hung from the rope she\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d attached to the ceiling fan, and all the blood vessels in her eyes burst.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 They were at the hospital because Jason and Kelly were having a daughter.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 They pushed as hard as they could, and after fourteen hours, Jason was holding his child. Kelly asked one of the attending nurses to fill her body with morphine, but the nurse said no, so Kelly, still bleeding, walked up to the hospital\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s roof and jumped off. She pancaked on the pavement in front of a wheelchair-bound grandmother, who cackled at her with cracked, mottled lips, and two black gravestones for teeth.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t know if I can keep doing this,\u8221\'94 Jason said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Zoey yanked at Kelly\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s hair and started climbing over her shoulder. \u8220\'93I understand.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s just been really hard for me, these past few years.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m sorry I didn\u8217\'92t see it.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I have so much responsibility with work.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I should have paid more attention.\u8221\'94 Zoey threw Kelly\u8217\'92s keys under the couch. She clapped and giggled.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 After Kelly cooked a beef stir fry, got Zoey to bed, and put the washing on, she fell asleep in her car, engine started and exhaust blocked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 The next year, Kelly found her pistol in the back of her dresser in her new one-bedroom apartment. She carried it with her when she caught the train into the city. Halfway to her stop, when the sun was warm on her back through the window, and a group of girls were giggling about the boys at school, Franklin sat himself down next to her.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Afternoon,\u8221\'94 Franklin said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hey,\u8221\'94 Kelly said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What\u8217\'92s up with that?\u8221\'94 he said, and nodded to her pistol.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m killing myself.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0
\par\plain 
\par\plain 
\par\plain {On to your questions:}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {- At a basic level, I think it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s completely fine with no scene breaks. But, I think this decision should be rooted in what exactly you want to do with this piece; are you wanting to convey a sense of a constant rumble of a relationship, or a push and pull, where in between scene breaks, Kelly is alright, but then she always gets worse, a cycle. IDK. It \u8216\'91works\u8217\'92 without the breaks.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {-  As for the suicides\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85from the first death, we already understand that she has absolutely no concern with her physical being. So the second death didn\u8217\'92t really add anything to that. Basically, I got the sense that you\u8217\'92re playing with a same bit of contrast over and over and over (dull/depressed/clueless relationship placed against the visceral intensity of the death). Maybe if there was a build in intensity of the deaths, that might make them more impactful? Or maybe it should be the opposite, start intense and then become more and more subdued as Kelly becomes more resigned to her depression. IDK. I didn\u8217\'92t feel much from them because there\u8217\'92s no arc to them.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {- I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m of a couple minds about Jason and Kelly as characters, which I think goes to show that, for me, the intent was not clear. At times, I was thinking that this was a situation where a mentally ill woman was being mentally ill, but her devoted (and stable) partner was intent on being there for her. But then elsewhere, Jason reads like he\u8217\'92s totally oblivious to the situation and doesn\u8217\'92t actually care about her at all. And then Kelly seems completely clueless even to her own situation. A little more on this later.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {- I think the lack of interiority can work. But if we\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re not being drawn into the piece via the character\u8217\'92s internal world, then I think the raw facts of the situation need to be super clear and consistent (e.g. \u8220\'93for sale, baby shoes, never worn\u8221\'94). Some of the facts in the story are unfortunately obscured behind some of the surrealist moves. Like, what the hell is the grandma supposed to be?}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {- What is the story about? I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m not exactly sure. But, for what it\u8217\'92s worth, here\u8217\'92s what I *want* the story to be about, and what I think(?) you are going for: A man approaches a woman in an art gallery with the intent of asking her out. He is unaware that she is severely mentally ill (BPD?). They form a relationship. Her mental illness plagues the relationship. He sticks through it because he loves her (or maybe he is completely oblivious? I think he\u8217\'92s oblivious due to the BMW and the Stocks, but I\u8217\'92m a liiiiitle confused because of things like, he watches her put cyanide in the coffee, which would show he is attentive). They get married, have a kid. In the end, the illness proves too much (or is it his work that is the problem?), it has worn the man down beyond his capacity to care for her. They separate. The piece ends with the woman meeting another unwary man in a park, and the cycle starts over. The piece is a surrealist peek into the arc of a tragic mental condition. }
\par\plain 
\par\plain {If this is indeed your intent, or close to it, I feel it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s missing one or two story beats, or maybe points of clarity about just who these people are and precisely how they feel about each other. And it\u8217\'92s got to be simple and clear and consistent because the piece is so short. It will be hard to parse otherwise (e.g. Kelly being turbo depressed/nothing matters, but then super materialistic with wondering how much the diamond was worth). I\u8217\'92m speaking declaratively there; I\u8217\'92m aware it\u8217\'92s just my opinion.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain 
\par\plain {Minor crits:}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {Pollock died in a car crash, not a gunshot wound. Do you mean, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93like \u8216\'91a\u8217\'92 Jackson Pollock\u8221\'94 ?}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {>After Kelly cooked a beef stir fry, got Zoey to bed, and put the washing on, she fell asleep in her car, engine started and exhaust blocked. }
\par\plain 
\par\plain {This sentence is clunker. Sooooo much disconnected information. Yes, it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s connected via contrast, but that doesn\u8217\'92t much help. The obvious solution is to break it into two sentences.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain {As for the rest of the writing, IMO it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s fine. G}{\f0\fs24\b0 ravestones for teeth is maybe too cliche, though I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be the first put against the wall for that crime.}
